# Chapter Six: The Weight of the Crown

The silence in Shiv’s study wasn't just quiet; it was suffocating. 

Shiv remained frozen behind his mahogany desk, his dark gray eyes locked onto KK. The revelation that the spoiled college brat Mandhira had slapped three days ago wasn't just a random street punk—he was Vicky, Bagga’s younger brother—changed the entire chessboard. 

"Are you certain?" Shiv’s voice dropped into a low, dangerous register that made even KK tighten his posture. 

"Positive, Sir," KK replied, sliding the detailed dossier forward. "We traced the financial backing on those college boys. It leads straight back to one of Bagga’s shell accounts. Vicky didn't just stumble into the twins; it was a targeted provocation. And when Mandhira put him in his place publicly, it humiliated them."

Shiv’s jaw clenched so hard a muscle ticked in his cheek. That explained why Bagga’s enforcers had been pushing into their transit routes near the Kali temple. It wasn't just territorial expansion—it was a personal war. Bagga was hunting for leverage against the Kashyaps, completely unaware that Shiv had inadvertently struck the ultimate counter-blow by rescuing Ira from his men just hours before. 

If Bagga found out Ira was hidden inside this mansion, and that Vicky was publicly humiliated by the Kashyap twins, it would trigger all-out war. 

"Double the perimeter guards," Shiv ordered coldly, standing up with a swift, controlled menace. "Bring all interior security feeds to my private monitor. And KK? Not a single word of this to my parents or the twins. They don't need to carry this weight."

"Understood, Sir. What about Miss Ira?" KK asked, hesitating at the door. "She’s safe inside the guest wing, but with the extra guards, she's going to know the net is tightening."

"I'll handle her," Shiv muttered, his chest tightening with a strange, unfamiliar dread. 
Upstairs, the heavy atmosphere of the study had bled into the hallways. Ira could see the new guards stationed near the stairwells, their sharp eyes scanning every shadow. The illusion of a safe, quiet sanctuary was shattering.

She couldn't stay here. Every minute she remained under this roof, she was dragging a violent, ruthless cartel straight to the doorstep of a family that had done nothing but show her kindness.

When a firm, measured knock sounded at her door, Ira didn't wait. She pulled it open, her face pale, her eyes flashing with a desperate, stubborn resolve.

Shiv stood in the doorway. He looked tired, the hard lines of his face carved from pure stress, yet his cold gray eyes instantly softened the moment they landed on her.

"You shouldn't be wandering the halls, Ira," Shiv said, his voice low and gravelly, carrying the exhaustion of a man fighting a war on two fronts. "Go back inside."

"No," Ira said, her voice shaking slightly, but holding firm. She stepped closer, blocking his path. "I need to leave, Shiv."

Shiv froze. The air between them instantly went dead. "What did you say?"

"You heard me," Ira pressed, her heart hammering against her ribs. She gestured sharply toward the hallway, toward the armed guards below. "I see the guards. I know something has shifted. Bagga is looking for me, and whatever is happening downstairs... it leads back to me. I've brought my war to your home, and I won't let you or your family pay for my survival."

Shiv’s jaw tightened. He took a slow, measured step forward, closing the distance between them until she was forced to tilt her head back to meet his piercing gaze. The sheer intensity rolling off him was terrifying.

"You think you can just walk out of these gates?" Shiv asked, his voice dangerously soft, a tremor of suppressed fury vibrating beneath the surface. "You think stepping out into Manali right now is some kind of noble sacrifice? Bagga’s men are tearing the town apart looking for you. The second you leave this property, you aren't protecting me, Ira—you're walking straight into an executioner's arms."

"And staying here makes you a target!" Ira fired back, tears of sheer frustration finally spilling over, though she refused to look away. "Why are you doing this? Why are you risking everything—your life, your family, your empire—for a stranger you found bleeding on a dirt road? It doesn't make sense!"

Shiv stared down at her, a fierce, suffocating storm brewing in his eyes. For a long, agonizing moment, neither of them breathed. The unsaid things—the dangerous, magnetic pull drawing them together against every logical instinct—hung heavy in the charged air.

Slowly, Shiv reached out. His large, warm hand wrapped firmly around her wrist, his thumb brushing gently over the edge of her bandage. His touch was an anchor in the middle of a hurricane.

"Because you stopped being a stranger the moment I looked at you," Shiv murmured, his voice dropping into a raw, gravelly whisper that cut straight through her soul. He didn't let go, holding her captive with eyes full of a fierce, terrifying devotion. "You think you're a burden? Let me worry about the weight of my own house, Ira. But don't you ever talk about walking out of my life again."

Ira’s breath hitched. Looking up into the hardened, uncompromising face of the man who had built walls of ice against the world just to shield her within them, the last of her defenses completely crumbled.
